[{:db/id "q-329336/field-1/a501ed2c68a0a753b5f653954a0333ef3fe1b5d9abb7b8f9f1f6a407662bdd3c" :answer/id #uuid "c103bcf0-a1f0-57e1-9d0c-aa462137be1f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{51}{128}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-329336/field-1/79d49c858b5edf1148886533a125b5456a31f60d21c5cf9cde2af3490363835f" :answer/id #uuid "56fd02dd-f41d-53f9-a8be-f314fb176330" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9}{32}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186220136 :answer-field/correct "q-329336/field-1/a501ed2c68a0a753b5f653954a0333ef3fe1b5d9abb7b8f9f1f6a407662bdd3c" :answer-field/choices ["q-329336/field-1/a501ed2c68a0a753b5f653954a0333ef3fe1b5d9abb7b8f9f1f6a407662bdd3c" "q-329336/field-1/79d49c858b5edf1148886533a125b5456a31f60d21c5cf9cde2af3490363835f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-329336"] :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x-2y+\\frac{3}{2},\\,y(1)=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2},$ and that one preliminary step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, complete the second row of the table below.\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\n*where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and ${y}_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.* \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{9}{16}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{17}{16}$ | $\\frac{7}{8}$ | $\\frac{9}{32}$ | $\\frac{43}{32}$ | $\\frac{13}{16}$ | {{field-1}} |" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nLooking at the partially filled table, we note the following:\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the current point $(x,y)=(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{17}{16})$ is ${y}^{′}=\\frac{7}{8}.$\n\n- From the first row, the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(1,\\frac{1}{2})$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is ${y}_{p}^{′}=\\frac{13}{16}.$\n\nNow, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot \\frac{13}{16}+8\\cdot \\frac{7}{8}-\\frac{3}{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}\\cdot \\frac{153}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{51}{128}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{9}{16}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{17}{16}$ | $\\frac{7}{8}$ | $\\frac{9}{32}$ | $\\frac{43}{32}$ | $\\frac{13}{16}$ | $\\frac{51}{128}$ |" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186220263 :answer-field/correct 17592186220266 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186220267 :answer-field/correct 17592186220268 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186220255 :answer-field/correct 17592186220256 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186220251 :answer-field/correct 17592186220252 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186220247 :answer-field/correct 17592186220250 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186220259 :answer-field/correct 17592186220261 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-332816"] :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x(2y-x-1),\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $Δx=1,$ and that one preliminary step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, complete the next row of the table below.\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\n*where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and ${y}_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.* \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} |\n\nTherefore, $y(2)≈$\n  {{field-6}}." :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nWe're told that the first step is calculated using the Runge-Kutta Method, resulting in \n\n$$\ny(1)≈\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll continue from this point, using the $2$-step ABM method.\n\nFirst, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x(2y-x-1) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (2\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}-1-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nFrom the first row of the table, we see that the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(0,1)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=0.$\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}-0)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $1$ |  |  |  |\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=\\frac{4}{3}+1=\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ={x}_{\\text{new}}(2{y}_{p}-{x}_{\\text{new}}-1) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (2\\cdot \\frac{7}{3}-2-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | $\\frac{10}{3}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot \\frac{10}{3}+8\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{4}{3})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot 22 \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | $\\frac{10}{3}$ | $\\frac{11}{6}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=1.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n{y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy=\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{11}{6}=\\frac{19}{6}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | $\\frac{10}{3}$ | $\\frac{11}{6}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\frac{19}{6}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈\\frac{19}{6}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-333095/field-1/e53026d2e72b5a9665afb169ba986753fff36dfc4557e460320cb8d9594cece8" :answer/id #uuid "65987ca5-6881-52ae-868b-bcd94ec27815" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333095/field-1/99f48c1b72e8eeab33eba2584ff0661456642ba13c82a72d98a76e8e1a07563e" :answer/id #uuid "c566cebd-498f-5854-b2b1-0e50be0840c5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333095/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "f1c6b895-dda4-5e2d-9679-2a9619f8ea4b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-333095/field-1/e53026d2e72b5a9665afb169ba986753fff36dfc4557e460320cb8d9594cece8" :answer-field/choices ["q-333095/field-1/e53026d2e72b5a9665afb169ba986753fff36dfc4557e460320cb8d9594cece8" "q-333095/field-1/99f48c1b72e8eeab33eba2584ff0661456642ba13c82a72d98a76e8e1a07563e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333095" :question/id #uuid "3f54c4f8-5ee5-5fbb-a2e3-e42e59e115dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-333095" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x+y,\\,y(0)=-1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{4},$ and that one preliminary step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, complete the second row of the table below.\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\n*where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and ${y}_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.* \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{5}{4}$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{3}{2}$ | $-1$ | {{field-1}} |" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nLooking at the partially filled table, we note the following:\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the current point $(x,y)=(\\frac{1}{4},-\\frac{5}{4})$ is ${y}^{′}=-1.$\n\n- From the first row, the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(0,-1)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=-1.$\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is ${y}_{p}^{′}=-1.$\n\nNow, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot (-1)+8\\cdot (-1)-(-1))\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}\\cdot (-12) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{5}{4}$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{3}{2}$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ |" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-333095/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-poi