 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333262/field-4/abe476e447976564bc5c670fe73789666287b967d49bbed12b1d85144449e63e" :answer/id #uuid "e2ebda04-07fc-5075-b89f-41aef1741d40" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "23" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333262/field-4/12b48b00f9c1ff14570ca66bc629787a88856efba1cda24cfb28da58c148801a" :answer/id #uuid "e7da9482-fe58-5366-a4a5-d9b89c09e13c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "22" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333262/field-4/f345fac9a81ccd096b9959c6e7a966a446e93e73b82d111a376f9064132509fd" :answer/id #uuid "ff57e55f-6067-5e84-9bbb-bc6974a6e4c5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "24" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333262/field-4" :answer-field/id #uuid "e41fdc2d-7e16-580d-a01b-2f1896584c0f" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-333262/field-4/f345fac9a81ccd096b9959c6e7a966a446e93e73b82d111a376f9064132509fd" :answer-field/choices ["q-333262/field-4/abe476e447976564bc5c670fe73789666287b967d49bbed12b1d85144449e63e" "q-333262/field-4/12b48b00f9c1ff14570ca66bc629787a88856efba1cda24cfb28da58c148801a" "q-333262/field-4/f345fac9a81ccd096b9959c6e7a966a446e93e73b82d111a376f9064132509fd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333262" :question/id #uuid "082d8cf1-a8e2-5c1e-8c15-edabaefc9968" :question/math-academy-id "q-333262" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2xy,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $Δx=1,$ and that one preliminary step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, complete the next row of the table below.\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\n*where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and ${y}_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.* \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{8}{3}$ | $\\frac{16}{3}$ | $8$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |\n\nTherefore, $y(2)≈$ {{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) Method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nFrom the second row of the partially filled table, we have that $y(1)≈\\frac{8}{3},$ where the predicted $y$-increment is $Δ{y}_{p}=8.$\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point given by the predictor equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=\\frac{8}{3}+8=\\frac{32}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{8}{3}$ | $\\frac{16}{3}$ | $8$ | $\\frac{32}{3}$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =2{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{p} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2\\cdot \\frac{32}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{128}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{8}{3}$ | $\\frac{16}{3}$ | $8$ | $\\frac{32}{3}$ | $\\frac{128}{3}$ |  |\n\nAlso from the table, we note the following:\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the current point $(x,y)=(1,\\frac{8}{3})$ is ${y}^{′}=\\frac{16}{3}.$\n\n- From the first row, the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(0,1)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=0.$\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot \\frac{128}{3}+8\\cdot \\frac{16}{3}-0)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot \\frac{768}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{8}{3}$ | $\\frac{16}{3}$ | $8$ | $\\frac{32}{3}$ | $\\frac{128}{3}$ | $\\frac{64}{3}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=2.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n{y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy=\\frac{8}{3}+\\frac{64}{3}=24\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{8}{3}$ | $\\frac{16}{3}$ | $8$ | $\\frac{32}{3}$ | $\\frac{128}{3}$ | $\\frac{64}{3}$ |\n| $2$ | $24$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈24.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-333262/field-1" "q-333262/field-2" "q-333262/field-3" "q-333262/field-4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f5aefa9-a83c-5203-8b73-c96b5f7df6bf"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-333262"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-333687/selection/693ce2e36726323cdeda13b3661a00f5789a09cb38857a5fd4b00e94aa461071" :answer/id #uuid "d93500d6-1069-5f7b-844b-f485697c7ae4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{97}{16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333687/selection/9a949048d2de1ca52a4989dc4111f1d6965c4a6b8df0e7247b005a0fc0d64736" :answer/id #uuid "ac18c951-6990-5883-9709-de365f28e9f2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{109}{16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333687/selection/599800a9942bfe57e7da32e069790a84c71933c2be4f6b010454f14dba1917d3" :answer/id #uuid "f1f736d9-9da7-50ee-a79b-f372dd290f43" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{113}{16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333687/selection/35c0ccb51507836af369d4d99af2defe070df6675a608cdbbb4a353a60b4d151" :answer/id #uuid "0522a605-4003-527b-a8a4-eeba2d31d5b4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{121}{16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333687/selection/bde4f83da3cf5a052305dd82b161b6af0b8db782750141991d915f026e475f9c" :answer/id #uuid "6fa6c69a-46e2-537b-9fff-93f89b4f3efe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{105}{16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333687/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "dae63ddd-40e2-5076-9004-0852a2184cc6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-333687/selection/599800a9942bfe57e7da32e069790a84c71933c2be4f6b010454f14dba1917d3" :answer-field/choices ["q-333687/selection/693ce2e36726323cdeda13b3661a00f5789a09cb38857a5fd4b00e94aa461071" "q-333687/selection/9a949048d2de1ca52a4989dc4111f1d6965c4a6b8df0e7247b005a0fc0d64736" "q-333687/selection/599800a9942bfe57e7da32e069790a84c71933c2be4f6b010454f14dba1917d3" "q-333687/selection/35c0ccb51507836af369d4d99af2defe070df6675a608cdbbb4a353a60b4d151" "q-333687/selection/bde4f83da3cf5a052305dd82b161b6af0b8db782750141991d915f026e475f9c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333687" :question/id #uuid "6ce77591-cec6-565a-a809-39e62243ef30" :question/math-academy-id "q-333687" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x-y-10,\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nTo approximate the solution, one step of the Runge-Kutta method has already been computed, resulting in the following estimate:\n\n$$\ny(2)≈-\\frac{9}{2}\n$$\n\nUse the $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton Method with one step to approximate $y(3).$\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\n*where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and ${y}_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.*" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM $2$-step) Method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nBefore we start, let's add the initial condition data and Runge-Kutta step data to a table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ |  | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{9}{2}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=3,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=3-2=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the values of ${y}^{′}$ at each point according to the given rule:\n\n- The slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(1,2)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{-1}^{′} & ={x}_{-1}-{y}_{-1}-10 \\\\  & =1-2-10 \\\\  & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The slope at the current point $(x,y)=(2,-\\frac{9}{2})$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x-y-10 \\\\  & =2-(-\\frac{9}{2})-10 \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-11$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{9}{2}$ | $-\\frac{7}{2}$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot (-\\frac{7}{2})-(-11))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-11$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{9}{2}$ | $-\\frac{7}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |  |  |  |\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=-\\frac{9}{2}+\\frac{1}{4}=-\\frac{17}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-11$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{9}{2}$ | $-\\frac{7}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{17}{4}$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ={x}_{\\text{new}}-{y}_{p}-10 \\\\  & =3-(-\\frac{17}{4})-10 \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-11$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{9}{2}$ | $-\\frac{7}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{17}{4}$ | $-\\frac{11}{4}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot (-\\frac{11}{4})+8\\cdot (-\\frac{7}{2})-(-11))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot (-\\frac{123}{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{41}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-11$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{9}{2}$ | $-\\frac{7}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{17}{4}$ | $-\\frac{11}{4}$ | $-\\frac{41}{16}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=3.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{2}+(-\\frac{41}{16}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{113}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-11$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{9}{2}$ | $-\\frac{7}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{17}{4}$ | $-\\frac{11}{4}$ | $-\\frac{41}{16}$ |\n| $3$ | $-\\frac{113}{16}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)≈-\\frac{113}{16}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-333687/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5a37e3d-f1ce-57f2-baef-c464a4f89ad4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-333687"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
